For clarity, I will note here that this is a different scenario than
mentioned both above and in the past.

> A: They can't follow at full speed.

Confusing and running from them is turns better spent just killing them.
If they're no threat to you, there's no point wasting time confusing
them.

I don't think Spork has changed that much from vanilla yet in this respect; 
there are only a few monsters that you're better off taking specific
countermeasures against rather than just ignoring or killing them.

Most monsters you can't ignore are better off dead if possible, usually
for very good reasons.  There are always exceptions, but usually if it
isn't worth ignoring, it needs to get dead.

> B: The rest of the critters are blocked until they kill that one beast.

Confusing them leads to them possibly moving out of the way of the
creatures behind them.  Unconfused creatures will stand obligingly right
behind you in a crowd, vigorously beating on you for 1 point of damage 
per turn.  Gehennom was opened up so that summon storms (common in
Gehennom) would actually matter more; in the old mazes, being hit by two
creatures at once was simply nowhere near more threatening than being
hit by only one from the front, and it allowed you to simply kill
through the storm on one side 'til you got a monster that didn't matter
(Disenchanters were favourite), then you just walked the other way.

(In vanilla, you can exacerbate this by dropping a gold piece, writing
dust E, and letting _it_ clog the entire hallway; I used this to seal
off Riders in visited Astral wings, since while @, A, and Riders can pass 
Elbereth, the summoned ants and random dragons cannot.)

